===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# That which results; the [[conclusion]] or end to which any course or condition of things leads, or which is obtained by any process or operation; consequence or effect.
#: {{hypo|en|primitive|sum|difference|product|qq4=mathematics}}
#: {{ux|en|the '''result''' of a course of action;&emsp; the '''result''' of a mathematical operation}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|last=Kołakowski|first=Leszek|authorlink=Leszek Kołakowski|title=w:Main Currents of Marxism|volume=I: The Founders|origlang=pl|original=Główne nurty marksizmu: powstanie|translator=P[aul] S[teven] Falla|origyear=1976|publisher=w:Clarendon Press|location=w:Oxford|isbn=9780198245476|oclc=181789472|page=372|passage=But neither poverty, nor uncontrolled production, nor a falling profit rate give ground for believing that capitalism 'must' collapse, still less that the '''result''' of its collapse must be a socialist society as described by Marx.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|title=No hiding place| date=2013-05-25| volume=407| issue=8837| page=74| url=http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21578357-plan-assess-peoples-personal-characteristics-their-twitter-streams-no| passage=In America alone, people spent $170 billion on “direct marketing”—junk mail of both the physical and electronic varieties—last year. Yet of those who received unsolicited adverts through the post, only 3% bought anything as a '''result'''. If the bumf arrived electronically, the take-up rate was 0.1%. And for online adverts the “conversion” into sales was a minuscule 0.01%.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20171025020559/https://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21578357-plan-assess-peoples-personal-characteristics-their-twitter-streams-no|archivedate=25 October 2017}}
# The final product, beneficial or tangible [[effect]](s) achieved by effort.
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=1| passage=The stories did not seem to me to touch life. They were plainly intended to have a bracing moral effect, and perhaps had this '''result''' for the people at whom they were aimed.}}
# The decision or determination of a council or deliberative assembly; a [[resolve]]; a [[decree]].
#: {{syn|en|conclusion|opinion|verdict|;|edict|resolution|ruling|Thesaurus:judgement}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|passage=Then of their session ended they bid cry / With trumpet's regal sound the great '''result'''.|year=1873}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A flying back; [[resilience]].
#* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|passage=Sound is produced between the string and the air by the return or the '''result''' of the string.}}
# {{lb|en|sports}} The final [[score]] in a game.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1935|author=[https://www.goodreads.com/author/show/288354.George_Goodchild George Goodchild]
|title=Death on the Centre Court|chapter=3
|passage=It had been his intention to go to Wimbledon, but as he himself said: “Why be blooming well frizzled when you can hear all the '''results''' over the wireless. And '''results''' are all that concern me.&nbsp;[&hellip;]”}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=September 24, 2011|author=David Ornstein|title=Arsenal 3 - 0 Bolton
|work=BBC Sport|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/14958156.stm
|passage=The Gunners boss has been heavily criticised for his side's poor start to the Premier League season but this '''result''' helps lift the pressure.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20111128154522/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/14958156.stm|archivedate=28 November 2011}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A positive or favourable [[outcome]] for someone.

